G ... when the environment cannot resolve a public counterpart'","sign_opcert_manifest is a second public mint entry point (src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py:586, re-exported at signing.py:100, exercised across many tests)","docs/event-schema.md:52 lists three SIGNATURE producers: rebar.signing.sign_manifest, rebar._opcert_signing.sign_opcert_manifest, and rebar._commands.remote_cert.persist_envelope","Problem statement: the defect class 'can regress the moment a new deployment shape puts the signing key somewhere the resolution chain does not reach' — which is not sign_manifest-specific","Scope section enumerates out-of-scope items but is silent on the sibling mint seams"],"finding":"AC1 scopes the mint-time warning to sign_manifest only, but two other op-cert mint seams that produce the same silently-uncertifiable attestation class — rebar.signing.sign_opcert_manifest and rebar._commands.remote_cert.persist_envelope — are neither included nor explicitly excluded.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f15ec2ba03c4ae278","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 1 / AC1 / Scope","norm_id":"n71d4a0f49241ba0c","priority":0.1257,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A cert minted via sign_opcert_manifest / remote_cert under a key whose public half cannot be resolved on the same box — no mint-time warning fires because the guard only sits on the sign_manifest / mint_opcert_record path"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Add an out-of-scope line: 'sign_opcert_manifest / remote_cert.persist_envelope, which mint under a CALLER-supplied key_path/principal rather than the ambient <tracker>/.opcert-key resolution chain _opcert_own_public_keys mirrors; the AC1 guard is specific to the ambient mint (mint_opcert_record) where that chain is authoritative.'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2857,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"na","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"incomplete_enumeration","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Justify (or narrow) AC1's mint-time condition: state the concrete case in which _opcert_own_public_keys returns empty despite the just-resolved private key and an available ssh-keygen, or redefine the mint check to compare the signing key's derived pub against the pinned trusted-environment key (the AC3 condition) so it catches the regression class the bug actually names.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.712,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan step 1: 'ask the verify-side resolver (_opcert_own_public_keys) whether a public counterpart is resolvable'","AC3 reframing: 'The op-cert public key is DERIVATIVE (_derive_opcert_pub = ssh-keygen -y, re-derivable on demand from the private key)'","_opcert_signing.py:261-272 _read_opcert_pub falls back to _derive_opcert_pub; :317 mint calls sshsig.ensure_available() before resolving the key","_opcert_own_public_keys is empty only if a private key path exists but derivation fails despite ssh-keygen being available (e.g. corrupt/non-Ed25519) — a narrow window the plan does not name"],"finding":"The AC1/AC2 mint-time guard condition ('the environment cannot resolve a public counterpart for the key it just signed under') may be effectively unreachable, because the public half is DERIVATIVE and re-derivable on demand: mint has just resolved/created the private key and already confirmed ssh-keygen is available (sshsig.ensure_available at _opcert_signing.py:317), and the verify-side resolver _opcert_own_public_keys falls back to _derive_opcert_pub (ssh-keygen -y) which re-derives the pub from that private key. The plan's own AC3 reframing states the pub is 're-derivable on demand,' which contradicts AC1's premise.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f79bdb82ff20b7fdc","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 1 (MINT) / AC1 / AC2","norm_id":"n503d130c9dfd2e92","priority":0.088,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A normal on-box mint where the private key exists and ssh-keygen works: _opcert_own_public_keys returns non-empty, so AC1's warning never fires even for a key the trusted-env pin does not reach"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3333,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"no","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"low","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Declare the AC3 `opcert` health-field payload explicitly (a bare degraded boolean/enum status vs. embedding the key path/principal), given the field lands on the unauthenticated `/health` route mounted outside auth middleware.","cohort":["T5c"],"criteria":["T5c"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6937,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["_mcp_health.py:16-18 — '/health is a FastMCP custom_route ... OUTSIDE the SDK RequireAuthMiddleware ... so an unauthenticated container HEALTHCHECK/probe still gets 200 even when auth is enabled'","_mcp_health.py:249 — store_status returns {path, present, expected} including a full path on this same unauthenticated route","plan AC1: warning 'naming the resolved key path and the principal' — establishes key path + principal as the surfaced identifiers","ABSENCE: AC3 says 'reports a DEGRADED opcert field' but does not specify the field's payload shape; the data at issue is non-secret (public key / path / principal), so this is advisory, not a plaintext-secret gap"],"finding":"AC3's DEGRADED `opcert` field is added to the `/health` route, which is deliberately mounted OUTSIDE auth middleware and answers unauthenticated probes, but the plan never declares what the field's payload contains — mirroring store_status (which returns a full filesystem `path`) plus the mint-side 'names the resolved key path and the principal' framing risks the field embedding key paths/principal identities visible to any unauthenticated prober.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc1bbed98b67f2f50","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 2 / AC3","norm_id":"ndef6bafc5f7a3005","priority":0.2063,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An unauthenticated actor curls /health on a public-facing MCP box and reads the opcert field, learning the signing-key filesystem path / principal identity of the deployment"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"State that the opcert health fie